pg_bp_lirs -- LIRS-2 Internal Design
=====================================

This document describes the internal design of the pg_bp_lirs extension
for PostgreSQL developers and maintainers.  It covers the algorithm
theory, data structures, access pattern handling, concurrency model,
memory layout, and known limitations.

Source file: contrib/pg_bp_lirs/pg_bp_lirs.c


A. Algorithm Theory -- LIRS
============================

LIRS (Low Inter-reference Recency Set) is a cache replacement algorithm
based on the concept of Inter-Reference Recency (IRR).

Inter-Reference Recency (IRR)
-----------------------------

The IRR of a page is the number of distinct pages accessed between two
consecutive accesses to the same page.  For example, if the access
sequence is A B C D A, then the IRR of page A is 3 (pages B, C, D were
accessed between the two accesses to A).

IRR is a more precise measure of a page's "hotness" than simple recency
(time since last access) because it captures how many other pages compete
for cache space between re-accesses.  A page with low IRR will be needed
again before many other pages are accessed; a page with high IRR may not
be needed again for a long time.

Page Classification
-------------------

Based on IRR, each page is classified as either:

  LIR (Low IRR) -- "Hot" pages.  These have short inter-reference gaps
      and are kept in the cache.  LIR pages are never direct eviction
      candidates; they must first be demoted to HIR.

  HIR (High IRR) -- "Cold" pages.  These have long inter-reference gaps
      and are eviction candidates.  Resident HIR pages live in the Q
      list.  Non-resident HIR pages become ghost entries.

Data Structures
---------------

The algorithm maintains three data structures:

  Stack S -- A recency-ordered doubly-linked list.  Contains all LIR
      entries, some resident HIR entries, and some ghost (non-resident
      HIR) entries.  The bottom of the stack (LRU end) represents the
      least recently accessed entry; the top (MRU end) represents the
      most recently accessed.

      The stack serves a dual purpose: (1) it tracks recency for LIR
      pages, and (2) it records whether an HIR page was accessed since
      the bottom-of-stack LIR page was last accessed.  An HIR page
      present in the stack has demonstrated a shorter IRR than the
      bottom LIR page and is eligible for promotion.

  Q List -- A doubly-linked list of resident HIR pages, ordered for
      eviction.  The head is the eviction end (oldest HIR); the tail
      is the insertion end (newest HIR).  Eviction always takes from
      the head.

  Ghost Set -- Non-resident HIR entries that remain in the stack after
      their data has been evicted.  These are also tracked in a ghost
      hash table for O(1) lookup on cache miss.  A ghost hit indicates
      that a page was recently evicted and should be promoted to LIR
      upon re-admission to the cache.


B. LIRS-2 Improvement
======================

The original LIRS algorithm (Jiang and Zhang, SIGMETRICS 2002) suffers
from unbounded stack growth.  Because ghost entries can accumulate in
the stack for every distinct page ever accessed, the stack can grow to
O(n) where n is the total number of distinct pages in the workload.
For database workloads that touch millions of pages over time, this is
impractical.

LIRS-2 (Jiang et al., HPL-2017-28, 2017) solves this by bounding the
stack to at most 2 * cache_size entries.

The pruning algorithm has two steps:

  Step 1 (Standard bottom prune): Remove non-LIR entries from the
      stack bottom until the bottom entry is an LIR page.  This
      maintains the standard LIRS invariant that the stack bottom
      should be an LIR page.

  Step 2 (LIRS-2 hard cap): If the stack still exceeds 2 * nbuffers
      entries, continue removing non-LIR entries from the bottom until
      the bound is satisfied or only LIR entries remain.

Ghost entries pruned from the stack during either step are freed
entirely: they are removed from the ghost hash table and returned to
the CDB free list.  Resident HIR entries removed from the stack remain
in Q (they just lose their stack membership and thus their eligibility
for promotion to LIR).

In the implementation, the max_stack_size field in LirsControl holds
the value 2 * nbuffers.  The lirs_stack_prune() function performs both
steps and is called after every stack modification.


C. Access Patterns
===================

The following describes how each type of page access is handled:

LIR Hit (hot page re-accessed)
------------------------------
Callback: LirsOnHit, status == LIRS_STATUS_LIR

  1. Move the LIR entry to the stack top (MRU position).
  2. Call lirs_stack_prune() to clean up the stack bottom.

This is analogous to an LRU move-to-front.  The prune ensures the
stack bottom invariant (bottom must be LIR) is maintained.

HIR Hit -- In Stack (cold page eligible for promotion)
------------------------------------------------------
Callback: LirsOnHit, status == LIRS_STATUS_HIR, in_stack == true

  1. Remove the entry from Q.
  2. Move the entry to the stack top.
  3. Change status from HIR to LIR.  Increment lir_count, decrement
     hir_count.
  4. Call lirs_stack_prune().
  5. If lir_count > lir_capacity, call lirs_demote_bottom_lir() to
     push the bottom-of-stack LIR page down to HIR (appended to Q).
  6. Call lirs_stack_prune() again after the demotion.

The key insight: this HIR page is in the stack, which means it was
accessed more recently than the bottom LIR page.  Its IRR is therefore
shorter than that of the bottom LIR page, so it deserves promotion.

HIR Hit -- Not In Stack (cold page, no promotion)
--------------------------------------------------
Callback: LirsOnHit, status == LIRS_STATUS_HIR, in_stack == false

  1. Remove the entry from its current Q position.
  2. Re-append it to the Q tail (most recently accessed HIR).
  3. Push the entry onto the stack top.
  4. Call lirs_stack_prune().

The entry stays as HIR.  Without prior stack membership, the algorithm
has no evidence that its IRR is shorter than the bottom LIR page's.
However, pushing it onto the stack means it will be eligible for
promotion on its next hit (if that hit occurs before it is pruned from
the stack bottom).

Ghost Hit (non-resident HIR re-accessed on miss)
-------------------------------------------------
Callback: LirsOnMiss sets ghost_cdb; LirsOnNewTag consumes it.

  On miss (LirsOnMiss):
    1. Look up the tag in the ghost hash table.
    2. If found and status == GHOST, save the CDB index in the
       per-backend ghost_cdb field for use by LirsOnNewTag.

  On new tag assignment (LirsOnNewTag, ghost_cdb >= 0):
    1. Remove the ghost entry from the ghost hash table.
    2. Set status to LIR.  Assign the new buffer ID.
    3. Move to stack top (the ghost may already be in the stack).
    4. Increment lir_count.
    5. Call lirs_stack_prune().
    6. If lir_count > lir_capacity, demote the bottom LIR to HIR.
    7. Prune again.

The ghost hit is strong evidence of a short IRR: the page was evicted
but accessed again before its ghost was pruned.  Direct promotion to
LIR is the correct response.

Complete Miss (page never seen or ghost already pruned)
-------------------------------------------------------
Callback: LirsOnNewTag, ghost_cdb == -1

  1. Allocate a new CDB entry from the free list (or recycle the
     oldest ghost from the stack bottom).
  2. If the cache is still warming up (lir_count < lir_capacity and
     total resident < nbuffers): insert as LIR, push onto stack.
  3. Otherwise: insert as HIR.
     a. If VACUUM hint is set: prepend to Q head (eviction end),
        do NOT push onto stack.
     b. Otherwise: append to Q tail, push onto stack.
  4. Call lirs_stack_prune().


D. Implementation Details
==========================

LirsCDB Structure
-----------------

Each Cache Directory Block (CDB) entry contains:

    buf_tag       BufferTag   -- page identity (reltablespace, relfilenode,
                                 forknum, blocknum)
    buf_id        int         -- pool-local buffer index (-1 for ghosts)
    status        int         -- LIRS_STATUS_LIR, _HIR, _GHOST, or _UNUSED
    in_stack      bool        -- true if entry is currently in stack S
    ghost_next    int         -- next entry in ghost hash chain (-1 = end)
    stack_prev    int         -- previous entry in stack (toward LRU/bottom)
    stack_next    int         -- next entry in stack (toward MRU/top)
    q_prev        int         -- previous entry in Q (toward head/eviction)
    q_next        int         -- next entry in Q (toward tail/insertion)

Each LirsCDB is roughly 64 bytes (BufferTag is the largest field at
roughly 20 bytes, plus 7 integer/bool fields plus padding).

CDB Count: 3x Per Buffer
-------------------------

The extension allocates 3 CDB entries per physical buffer:

    ncdb = nbuffers * 3

This is 50% more than ARC/CAR's 2x multiplier.  The extra entries are
needed because LIRS can have up to nbuffers resident entries (LIR + HIR)
plus ghost entries in the bounded stack (up to 2 * nbuffers stack
entries, minus the resident LIR entries).  The 3x factor provides
sufficient CDB slots for the worst-case mix of resident and ghost
entries.

Unused CDB entries are maintained on a singly-linked free list (chained
via stack_next).  When the free list is empty, the allocator scans the
stack bottom for the oldest ghost entry to recycle.

Stack Implementation
--------------------

Stack S is a doubly-linked list using integer indices into the CDB array
(not pointers).  LirsControl maintains:

    stack_top     -- index of MRU entry (-1 if empty)
    stack_bottom  -- index of LRU entry (-1 if empty)
    stack_size    -- current number of entries

Operations:
    lirs_stack_push()        -- insert at top (MRU end)
    lirs_stack_remove()      -- unlink from current position
    lirs_stack_move_to_top() -- remove + push (combined operation)
    lirs_stack_prune()       -- LIRS-2 bottom pruning (see section B)

All stack operations are O(1) except prune, which is O(k) where k is
the number of non-LIR entries removed from the bottom.

Q List Implementation
---------------------

Q is a doubly-linked list of resident HIR pages.  LirsControl maintains:

    q_head   -- eviction end, oldest HIR (-1 if empty)
    q_tail   -- insertion end, newest HIR (-1 if empty)
    q_size   -- current number of entries

Operations:
    lirs_q_append()   -- insert at tail (normal insertion)
    lirs_q_prepend()  -- insert at head (VACUUM pages, for fast eviction)
    lirs_q_remove()   -- unlink from current position

All Q operations are O(1).

Ghost Hash Table
----------------

The ghost hash table provides O(1) lookup of non-resident HIR entries
by BufferTag.  It follows the same open-chaining pattern used in the
ARC and CAR extensions:

  - Hash table size is the next power of 2 >= ncdb (minimum 64).
  - Each bucket is an integer index into the CDB array (-1 = empty).
  - Chaining uses the ghost_next field of LirsCDB.
  - Hash function: tag_hash() with power-of-2 masking.

Operations:
    lirs_ghost_hash_bucket() -- compute bucket index
    lirs_ghost_lookup()      -- find CDB by tag
    lirs_ghost_insert()      -- prepend to bucket chain
    lirs_ghost_remove()      -- unlink from bucket chain

LIR Capacity Scaling
---------------------

The lir_capacity field determines the maximum number of LIR (hot) pages.
It is set at initialization time based on pool size:

    Pool size (buffers)    LIR capacity
    -------------------    ------------
    < 128                  75% of nbuffers
    128 - 511              85% of nbuffers
    512 - 2047             90% of nbuffers
    2048 - 8191            95% of nbuffers
    >= 8192                99% of nbuffers

Smaller pools need proportionally more HIR slots to avoid running out
of eviction candidates under concurrent load (for example, autovacuum
plus benchmark queries competing for a small number of buffers).

The LIR capacity is fixed at init time.  Unlike ARC's target_T1_size,
it does not adapt dynamically at runtime.

LIRS-2 Stack Bound
-------------------

    max_stack_size = 2 * nbuffers

This is the hard upper bound on stack S entries, per the LIRS-2 paper.

Memory Layout in DSM
---------------------

The shared memory segment for each LIRS pool is laid out as:

    Offset 0:                              LirsControl structure
    sizeof(LirsControl):                   LirsCDB[ncdb]  (3 * nbuffers entries)
    sizeof(LirsControl) + ncdb*sizeof(CDB): int[ghost_hash_size]  (power-of-2)
    ... + ghost_hash_size*sizeof(int):      int[nbuffers]  (buf_to_cdb mapping)

All sections are MAXALIGN'd.  The total size is computed by
LirsShmemSize() and allocated during LirsShmemInit().

Buffer-to-CDB Mapping
----------------------

The buf_to_cdb array maps pool-local buffer indices to CDB indices.
This provides O(1) lookup of a buffer's CDB entry during on_hit and
on_evict callbacks.  Entries are -1 when the buffer has no CDB (free
or untracked).


E. VACUUM Integration
======================

VACUUM pages receive special treatment to prevent cache pollution:

  1. LirsHintVacuum() sets the vacuum_hint flag in the per-backend
     LirsBackendState when VACUUM begins and clears it when VACUUM
     ends.

  2. In LirsOnNewTag(), if vacuum_hint is true, the new HIR page is
     inserted at Q head (eviction end) via lirs_q_prepend() instead
     of Q tail.

  3. The page is NOT pushed onto the stack.

This means VACUUM pages are:
  - First in line for eviction (Q head position).
  - Ineligible for promotion to LIR (no stack membership).
  - Effectively "use once and discard."

Without this, a large VACUUM operation could fill the cache with
transient pages, displacing hot LIR pages from the Q and causing
unnecessary demotion/promotion churn.


F. Ghost-Aware Prefetch
=========================

The LirsPrefetchHint() callback integrates LIRS with the buffer
manager's prefetch infrastructure:

  1. The buffer manager calls prefetch_hint with an array of BufferTags
     representing pages that will be accessed soon.

  2. For each tag, LIRS looks up the ghost hash table.

  3. If a ghost entry is found and it is in the stack, it is moved to
     the stack top via lirs_stack_move_to_top().

This serves two purposes:

  - Prevents premature pruning.  Without the prefetch hint, a ghost
    entry near the stack bottom could be pruned before the actual
    access arrives, converting what should be a ghost hit (with LIR
    promotion) into a complete miss.

  - Pre-positions the ghost for correct promotion.  When the real
    access arrives and triggers LirsOnMiss/LirsOnNewTag, the ghost
    entry is at the stack top, correctly reflecting its upcoming short
    IRR.

Only ghost entries already in the stack are moved.  Ghost entries not
in the stack (those that were pruned earlier) are not re-inserted,
as the prefetch hint alone is not strong enough evidence of a short
IRR to justify re-admission.


G. Victim Selection (LirsGetVictim)
=====================================

LirsGetVictim selects a buffer to evict.  It proceeds in three phases
with a retry loop:

Phase 1: Free Buffer Scan
--------------------------
If lir_count + hir_count < nbuffers, some buffers have no CDB entry
(they are free/untracked from cache warm-up).  Scan all buffers
looking for one with refcount == 0.  No lock is needed for this scan.

If an unpinned free buffer is found, pin it and return immediately.

Phase 2: Q Head Walk
---------------------
Acquire the LIRS spinlock and walk Q from head (oldest resident HIR)
toward tail:

  1. For each Q entry, verify it is still a resident HIR page with a
     valid buf_id.

  2. Read the buffer header state.  If refcount == 0 and not BM_LOCKED,
     attempt a compare-and-swap to pin the buffer.  On success, release
     the spinlock and return.

  3. If the buffer header is BM_LOCKED, release the spinlock, call
     WaitBufHdrUnlocked() (which may block), then reacquire the
     spinlock.  After reacquiring, re-validate the CDB entry because
     the Q list may have been modified while the lock was released.
     If the CDB entry changed (evicted, freed, or status changed),
     abandon this candidate and advance to the next Q entry.

  4. If refcount != 0 (buffer is pinned by another backend), skip to
     the next Q entry.

The maximum number of Q walk attempts is nbuffers * 3 to prevent
infinite loops under pathological conditions.

Phase 3: Fallback Full Scan
-----------------------------
If no HIR victim was found (all Q entries are pinned), fall back to
scanning ALL buffer descriptors in the pool, including LIR pages.
This is an emergency measure and should be rare in normal operation.

Retry Loop
-----------
If all three phases fail (every buffer in the pool is pinned), sleep
for 1ms and retry.  This handles the transient all-pinned scenario
that can occur under heavy concurrent load on small pools.  The retry
limit is 50 attempts (50ms total sleep).  After 50 failures, raise
an ERROR.


H. Concurrency
================

Lock Design
-----------

A single spinlock (lirs_lock in LirsControl) protects all mutable
fields: the stack, Q list, ghost hash table, all counters, and CDB
state transitions.

This is the same design as ARC.  Every LIR hit acquires the spinlock
to perform a stack move-to-top, making it the primary scalability
bottleneck under high concurrency.  CAR avoids this by using an atomic
reference bit for hits, only acquiring a lock during clock sweep.

Q Walk During get_victim
-------------------------

The Q head walk in LirsGetVictim must handle the case where a buffer
header is BM_LOCKED by another backend:

  1. Release the LIRS spinlock (to avoid holding it while blocking).
  2. Call WaitBufHdrUnlocked() to wait for the header lock.
  3. Reacquire the LIRS spinlock.
  4. Re-validate the CDB entry (status, buf_id) because another
     backend may have evicted or promoted the page during the window.

This release-wait-reacquire pattern avoids deadlock between the LIRS
spinlock and buffer header locks, at the cost of potential wasted work
if the CDB entry changed.

Statistics
----------

Per-pool statistics (lookups, hits, misses, etc.) use PostgreSQL's
pg_atomic_uint64 counters in the LirsControl structure.  Backends
accumulate counts in thread-local lirs_local_stats[] and flush them to
the shared atomics via PoolStatIncrement(), which batches updates to
reduce atomic operation overhead.


I. Memory Overhead
===================

Per-buffer metadata cost:

    Component                Size per buffer     Notes
    ---------                ---------------     -----
    LirsCDB entries          ~192 bytes          3 entries * ~64 bytes each
    Ghost hash               ~4 bytes            1 int per CDB, amortized
    buf_to_cdb               ~4 bytes            1 int per buffer
    LirsControl overhead     negligible          Fixed size, shared once

    Total per buffer:        ~200 bytes

This is the most expensive of the three algorithms:

    Algorithm    CDB multiplier    Approx. per-buffer overhead
    ---------    --------------    ---------------------------
    ARC          2x                ~130 bytes
    CAR          2x                ~130 bytes
    LIRS-2       3x                ~200 bytes

For a pool of 131072 buffers (1 GB at 8 KB pages), LIRS metadata
consumes roughly 25 MB versus roughly 17 MB for ARC/CAR.


J. Performance Characteristics
================================

Best-Case Workloads
-------------------

  - Cyclic/looping access patterns: LIRS correctly identifies the loop
    body as LIR even when the loop size slightly exceeds the cache.
    ARC/CAR tend to thrash because their recency-based eviction cannot
    distinguish loop pages from random one-shots.

  - Variable reuse distances: workloads where different pages have
    different but stable re-access intervals.  LIRS's IRR tracking
    naturally keeps the short-interval pages hot.

Worst-Case Workloads
---------------------

  - Purely random access: all algorithms degrade equally to roughly
    cache_size / total_pages hit ratio.  LIRS adds more overhead than
    CAR for the same result.

  - Bursty stack pruning: certain access patterns can cause many ghost
    entries to accumulate near the stack bottom, leading to a burst of
    pruning work on a single access.  The cost is O(k) where k is the
    number of entries pruned, bounded by the stack size.

Complexity of Key Operations
-----------------------------

    Operation                     Time Complexity
    ---------                     ---------------
    LIR hit (stack move + prune)  O(1) amortized, O(k) worst case
    HIR hit in stack (promote)    O(1) amortized, O(k) worst case
    HIR hit not in stack          O(1)
    Ghost hit (on_miss + new_tag) O(1) amortized, O(k) worst case
    Complete miss                 O(1) amortized
    Q-head eviction               O(1) when Q head is unpinned
    Ghost hash lookup             O(1) expected (hash table)
    Stack prune                   O(k) where k = non-LIR entries pruned

    k is bounded by max_stack_size (2 * nbuffers) in the worst case,
    but in practice is typically small (single digits).


