L. Known Limitations
=====================

1. Spinlock on every hit.  Same scalability limitation as ARC.  Every
   LIR hit (the common case for a well-tuned pool) must acquire the
   LIRS spinlock to perform a stack move-to-top operation.  Under high
   concurrency, this serializes all cache hits through a single lock.
   CAR's atomic reference bit approach avoids this bottleneck.

2. 3x CDB memory.  LIRS uses 50% more metadata memory than ARC or CAR.
   For very large pools (tens of thousands of buffers), this difference
   is measured in megabytes and may matter on memory-constrained systems.

3. Bursty stack pruning.  Under workloads that rapidly cycle through
   many distinct pages, ghost entries accumulate near the stack bottom.
   A subsequent access that triggers pruning may need to remove many
   entries in a single operation, causing a latency spike while the
   spinlock is held.

4. Fixed LIR capacity ratio.  The lir_capacity is computed once at pool
   initialization based on pool size and never changes.  ARC dynamically
   adjusts target_T1_size in response to ghost hit patterns, allowing
   it to adapt its recency/frequency balance at runtime.  LIRS has no
   equivalent runtime adaptation of the LIR/HIR partition.  If the
   optimal ratio changes over the lifetime of the workload, LIRS cannot
   adjust.

5. No per-table or per-relation awareness.  Like ARC and CAR, LIRS
   operates at the buffer (page) level without knowledge of which
   relation a page belongs to.  All pages in a pool compete equally
   for LIR status regardless of their relation.
